{"name":"Barbarian Leather","hex":"#a17308","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"barbarian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Relating to people, countries, or customs perceived as uncivilized or inferior."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A non-Greek or a non-Roman citizen.","An uncivilized or uncultured person, originally compared to the hellenistic Greco-Roman civilisation; usually associated with senseless violence and self-harm or other such shows of brute force and lack of mental faculty."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/barbarian"},{"term":"leather","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tough material produced from the skin of animals, by tanning or similar process, used e.g. for clothing; often denotes leather from cattle when no qualifier specifies otherwise.","A piece of the above used for polishing."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of leather.","Referring to one who wears leather clothing (motorcycle jacket, chaps over 501 jeans, boots), especially as a sign of sadomasochistic homosexuality."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/leather"}],"entries":[{"title":"Barbarian","blocks":[{"text":"A barbarian is, etymologically, a foreigner, specifically someone whose language and customs differ from those of the speaker. In ancient Greece, the term designated non-Greeks, while in the Roman world it referred more generally to peoples living outside the cultural and political sphere of the empire. In modern English, the word has developed a pejorative sense, commonly meaning a \"rude, wild, or uncivilized person\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Visigoths_sack_Rome.jpg/330px-Visigoths_sack_Rome.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barbarian","wikidata":"Q134313"},{"title":"Leather","blocks":[{"text":"Leather is a strong, flexible and durable material obtained from the tanning, or chemical treatment, of animal skins and hides to prevent decay. The most common leathers come from cattle, sheep, goats, equine animals, buffalo, pigs and hogs, ostriches, and aquatic animals such as seals and alligators."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Leathertools.jpg/330px-Leathertools.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leather","wikidata":"Q286"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Barbarian_prisoner_MAN_Napoli_Inv6116.jpg/500px-Barbarian_prisoner_MAN_Napoli_Inv6116.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Barbarian_prisoner_MAN_Napoli_Inv6116.jpg/1280px-Barbarian_prisoner_MAN_Napoli_Inv6116.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Barbarian_prisoner_MAN_Napoli_Inv6116.jpg/500px-Barbarian_prisoner_MAN_Napoli_Inv6116.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A preconnesian marble depiction of a barbarian. Second century AD.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABarbarian_prisoner_MAN_Napoli_Inv6116.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Alaric_entering_Athens.jpg/500px-Alaric_entering_Athens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Alaric_entering_Athens.jpg/500px-Alaric_entering_Athens.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"20th-century painting of Alaric I, leader of the Visigoths 395–410, entering Athens after capturing the city in 395","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlaric_entering_Athens.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Dying